<BaseLayout title="How to Find Your First Workflow to Automate | AI Workflow Toolkit" description="Choose a useful first automation by looking at repetition, predictable rules, error costs and exceptions.">
  <div class="wrap"><article class="article"><div class="page-hero"><span class="eyebrow">Automation basics · 7 min read</span><h1>Find your first workflow <span class="gradient">to automate.</span></h1><p>The best first automation is usually not the most impressive one. It is a small, frequent process with clear rules and an easy way to notice when something goes wrong.</p></div>
  <h2>Look for repeated work, not AI opportunities</h2><p>List the tasks you or your team repeat every week: copying form details into a project board, sending routine reminders, preparing meeting summaries or notifying a teammate when a new request arrives. A process does not need AI to be worth automating.</p>
  <h2>Score each candidate</h2><ul><li><strong>Frequency:</strong> How often does it happen?</li><li><strong>Time:</strong> How many minutes does each run consume?</li><li><strong>Consistency:</strong> Are the steps and rules mostly predictable?</li><li><strong>Risk:</strong> What happens if the automation makes a mistake?</li><li><strong>Exceptions:</strong> How often does a person need to make a judgment call?</li></ul>
  <h2>Start with a visible, reversible step</h2><p>Good early candidates include drafting a task from a new form submission, reminding a team member about an approaching deadline or creating a first draft of a weekly report. Keep a person in control of sending external messages or changing important records until the workflow has been tested.</p>
  <h2>Map the workflow before choosing a tool</h2><p>Write down the trigger, required inputs, actions, expected result and exceptions. Decide what should happen if a service is unavailable, a field is missing or the same event arrives twice. This simple map will make it easier to compare no-code automation tools or a small custom script.</p>
  <h2>Measure the result</h2><p>Record a baseline: time spent per week, number of errors or how long requests wait. Run the new workflow on a small sample, compare results and ask the people who use it whether it actually helps. Keep a manual fallback.</p>
  <div class="article-callout"><strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> Automate a stable process first. Redesign a confusing process before adding automation to it.</div>
